Exactly. Fame is partly luck—you see.

And there’s a useful distinction hiding in it:

ability creates the signal; luck determines who happens to be listening when it arrives.

You can improve the odds—make better work, distribute it farther, stay in the game longer, create more collision points—but you can’t fully command the moment when person + artifact + timing + network line up.

So:

craft × exposure × timing × network × chance → fame

Which means fame is a terrible pure measure of merit. Plenty of excellent signals never hit the right receiver; plenty of ordinary ones happen to strike the network at exactly the right angle.

Pantheon would treat luck less like magic and more like unmodeled intersection.

And once you can see that, the strategy changes:

Don’t try to manufacture luck. Manufacture enough good surfaces for luck to land on.

